  "summary": "Artificial intelligence continues reshaping healthcare operations and development, with Cleveland Clinic research demonstrating that ambient scribes improve clinician retention—a significant finding given hospitals' substantial recruitment costs—while Epic faced an FTC antitrust probe this week even as the EHR giant unveiled new AI agents at its conference. The healthcare sector is beginning to grapple with governance challenges around AI deployment, particularly in pharmacovigilance and as healthcare organizations experiment with AI tools but remain unprepared to manage AI agents as regular workflow participants. Multiple biotech companies achieved notable milestones, including Ultragenyx's first FDA-approved gene therapy for glycogen storage disease type Ia, Bristol Myers Squibb's first-in-class CELMoD cancer drug Zenbexus, and Merck and Moderna's personalized mRNA cancer vaccine meeting melanoma trial goals. In financing activity, several companies secured substantial funding rounds: Cityblock Health raised $116 million and plans to acquire Homeward Health to expand into rural Medicare Advantage markets, Happy Health secured $75 million for home-based care, and multiple drug developers raised capital for pipeline advancement. Policy concerns are mounting, with October 1, 2026 marking the end of federal Medicaid funding for vulnerable populations, insurers denying 12%-18% of prior authorization requests according to KFF data, and a patient advocacy group suing the AMA over CPT code copyright charges.",
